@model Edulytics.Core.Curriculum.LessonContent.RichLessonV2

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L01")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l01 aria-label="Grade 7 scaled flags and percentages">
    <h3>Scaled Flags and Percent Area — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>1.1 Preserve shape while changing size</h4>
    <p>Compare rectangular flags with different dimensions. Decide which are scaled copies by checking a common multiplicative factor for corresponding lengths.</p>
    <h4>1.2 Percent of a rectangular region</h4>
    <p>For a flag with a rectangular subregion, express the subregion area as a fraction of the whole and convert that fraction to a percentage.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A 6-by-10 flag is scaled so its width becomes 15. Find the new height. A 3-by-4 rectangle inside the original flag is highlighted. What percentage of the original flag area is highlighted? Explain why scaling the entire flag preserves that percentage.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the scale-factor explanation, area model and justification that corresponding area fractions are preserved. Machine Practice checks exact scale-factor and fraction-to-percent subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L02")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l02 aria-label="Grade 7 fractional ratios and rates">
    <h3>Rates with Fractions — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>2.1 Compare by one unit</h4><p>Convert fractional distance-time and recipe ratios to unit rates. Keep units attached to every quotient and compare rates only after the units agree.</p>
    <h4>2.2 Scale a proportional image</h4><p>Use a fractional scale factor to find missing dimensions and explain why multiplying corresponding lengths by one factor preserves the shape.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Runner A travels 2.25 miles in 18 minutes. Runner B travels 1.75 miles in 15 minutes. Find each rate in miles per minute and decide who is faster. Then state the reciprocal rate for the faster runner in minutes per mile.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check unit interpretation, comparison reasoning and the reciprocal-rate explanation. Machine Practice checks exact fractional rate subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L03")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l03 aria-label="Grade 7 revisiting proportional relationships">
    <h3>Revisit Proportional Relationships — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>3.1 Move among representations</h4><p>For one constant-rate situation, identify the unit rate, complete a table, write an equation, and explain what the constant means in context.</p>
    <h4>3.2 Use the inverse rate when useful</h4><p>When a problem asks how much input is needed for one output, switch to the reciprocal unit rate and state its units explicitly.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Bulk walnuts cost 18 for 7.5 pounds. Find dollars per pound and pounds per dollar, write an equation for cost c in terms of pounds p, and find the cost of 12 pounds.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check units, interpretation of the constant and the choice of forward versus reciprocal rate. Machine Practice checks exact rate, table and equation subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L04")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l04 aria-label="Grade 7 fractional increases and decreases">
    <h3>Describe Changes with Fractions — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>4.1 Connect words, addition, and multiplication</h4><p>Translate statements such as "half as much again" into both additive and multiplicative forms, and explain why x + 1/2 x = 3/2 x.</p>
    <h4>4.2 Increase versus decrease</h4><p>Use tape diagrams or equations to distinguish adding a fraction of the original from subtracting a fraction of the original. Keep the original quantity as the reference.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A skating route is 24 km. A longer route is 3/8 more than the original and a shorter route is 1/4 less than the original. Find both route lengths and write one equation for each change using the original length.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the reference quantity, equation interpretation, and explanation of why the multiplier is 1 plus or minus the fractional change. Machine Practice checks exact fractional-change subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L05")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l05 aria-label="Grade 7 decimal change factors">
    <h3>Say the Change with Decimals — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>5.1 Convert the change, not the reference</h4><p>Convert a fractional increase or decrease to a decimal, then combine it with 1 to form the multiplier on the original amount.</p>
    <h4>5.2 Explain equivalent equations</h4><p>Connect forms such as x+0.5x and 1.5x, and distinguish a terminating decimal from a repeating decimal when converting fractions.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A reader finishes 80 pages. A second reader finishes 0.375 more than that amount. Find the second amount, write both additive and multiplicative equations, and state the fraction equivalent to 0.375.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check equivalence among fraction, decimal, diagram and equation representations. Machine Practice checks exact conversion and change-factor subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L06")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l06 aria-label="Grade 7 percent increase and decrease">
    <h3>Percent Increase and Decrease — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>6.1 Keep 100% anchored to the original</h4><p>Use a tape diagram to show an original amount as 100%, then add or remove the stated percentage of that original amount.</p>
    <h4>6.2 Separate change from final amount</h4><p>Calculate the percent-change amount first, then combine it with the original. Explain why the final amount is more or less than 100% of the original.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A fish population is 640. It decreases by 15%. Find the decrease and the new population. Then write the new population as a percentage of the original.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the reference quantity, tape diagram and distinction between percent change and final percent. Machine Practice checks exact percentage calculations only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U04:L07")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u04-l07 aria-label="Grade 7 recover the original amount">
    <h3>Find the 100% Reference — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>7.1 Work backward from a changed amount</h4><p>When a final value represents 110%, 80%, or another percentage of the original, divide by the multiplier to recover the 100% reference.</p>
    <h4>7.2 Use a double number line</h4><p>Place the final percentage and final amount together, then scale to 100%. Check the result by applying the change forward again.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A detergent bottle now holds 660 mL after a 10% increase. Find the original volume. A second product is 480 g after a 20% decrease. Find its original mass and verify both answers forward.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check which quantity is the 100% reference and the backward reasoning. Machine Practice checks exact reverse-percent subresults only.</p>
  </section>
}
